 why the deck deserves\nattention.\n\nThe ending should resolve the opening and match the deck's purpose:\n\n- Decision decks end with a recommendation, decision, or explicit ask.\n- Action decks end with owners, next steps, or commitments.\n- Educational decks end with synthesis or application.\n- Exploratory and facilitation decks end with implications and productive\n  questions without prematurely forcing a conclusion.\n\nDo not end abruptly on a detail slide, technical artifact, unframed summary, or\ngeneric \"Thank you\" slide.\n\n## Write natural, audience-facing copy\n\nVisible slide copy must serve the intended audience. Do not expose the process\nused to create the deck, instructions to the model, private planning notes,\npresenter tips, talk tracks, or implementation details unless the user\nexplicitly asks for them in the deliverable.\n\nTreat a requested presentation duration as a planning constraint for scope,\nslide count, and pacing. Do not convert it into visible timestamps or time\nranges in slide titles. Include timings only when the user explicitly requests\na timed agenda, run of show, facilitator guide, or similar audience-facing\nschedule. When timings are useful only to the presenter, keep them in speaker\nnotes or separate facilitator material rather than visible slide titles.\n\nWhen the user says that a concept is irrelevant, out of scope, or should be\nremoved, remove it. Do not preserve the content in a slide that announces that\nthe concept is irrelevant, omitted, or outside the presentation. Include an\nexplicit scope boundary only when the audience needs it or the user requests\none.\n\nWrite titles and body copy in direct, specific, natural language:\n\n- Prefer concrete claims over vague slogans.\n- Avoid formulaic transformations such as \"From X to Y\" unless that contrast is\n  genuinely the clearest description of the change.\n- Do not use colons, semicolons, em dashes, or parallel constructions as a\n  repeated sentence template.\n- Avoid compressed fragments that resemble advertising taglines rather than\n  complete thoughts.\n- Replace jargon, abstract noun clusters, and inflated phrasing with concrete\n  nouns and active verbs.\n- Remove duplicated qualifiers and repeated concepts.\n- Vary sentence structure naturally instead of forcing every title or bullet\n  into the same rhetorical pattern.\n\nA title should communicate a point a human presenter would plausibly say aloud.\nIf it sounds like a prompt, production note, slogan generator, or mechanical\ntemplate, rewrite it.\n# Template-Following Mode\n\nUse when the user provides an existing PPTX, asks to follow a presentation, or\nattaches a PPTX that is clearly or implicitly a template.\n\nThis is the entire template-following mode. Do not run template codegen, do not\nbuild or consume a reusable template registry, and do not rebuild a fresh deck\nfrom palette, fonts, screenshots, or vibes. Use a source slide inventory only:\ninspect every source slide, choose source slides for the requested output,\nduplicate those source slides, and edit the copied elements in place.\n\nStore every template-following intermediate named in this reference under\n`$TMP_DIR`. Only `FINAL_PPTX` may be written outside `$TMP_DIR`.\n\n## Exact Clone/Edit Contract\n\n1. Copy/import the source PPTX.\n2. Inspect every source slide render and layout.\n3. Create `template-frame-map.json` mapping every output slide to a source\n   slide.\n4. Build `template-starter.pptx` by duplicating mapped source slides.\n5. Apply the requested edits while preserving the source deck's structure and\n   styling.\n6. Preserve the source template's typography exactly: keep original font\n   family, font size, weight, line spacing, paragraph spacing, text insets,\n   alignment, and vertical anchor for every edited text box/table cell unless\n   the user explicitly asks to restyle or resize. If new copy does not fit,\n   shorten the copy, choose a more suitable source layout, or split content\n   across another cloned slide; do not silently shrink text to make it fit.\n7. Audit inherited placeholders, including `sldNum`, `dt`, and `ftr`, and\n   either fill them intentionally or delete them. Treat visible default prompt\n   text such as `Slide Number`, `Date`, and `Footer` as an empty inherited\n   placeholder; never leave empty PowerPoint placeholders in the final deck,\n   even if PNG renders hide them.\n8. Export PNGs, layout JSON, and PPTX for QA.\n9. Deliver the edited copy and mention the sources cited or used.\n\nThe production load/export path is artifact-tool:\n\n```js\nconst presentation = await PresentationFile.importPptx(await FileBlob.load(sourcePptx));\nconst pptx = await PresentationFile.exportPptx(presentation);\nawait pptx.save(outputPptx);\n```\n\nUse artifact-tool and headless package tooling only. If artifact-tool cannot\ninspect, duplicate, render, or export the source deck, report the blocker. Do\nnot fall back to a theme-matched rebuild.\n\n## Source Slide Inventory\n\nBefore planning final copy, run:\n\n```bash\nnode \"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/inspect_template_deck.mjs\" \\\n  --workspace \"$TMP_DIR\" \\\n  --pptx \"<source.pptx>\"\n```\n\nReview all source slide PNGs, layout JSON files, `template-inspect.ndjson`,\nextracted media, font evidence, and `template-manifest.json`. Do not sample only\none or two representative slides.\n\nThen create under `$TMP_DIR`:\n\n- `template-audit.txt`: source structure, reusable slide types, inherited\n  placeholders, brand/assets, typography/spacing rules, and insertion contract.\n- `template-frame-map.json`: the full source slide inventory and the selected\n  source slide for every output slide.\n- `deviation-log.txt`: each intentional departure from a copied source slide,\n  with reason and affected slides.\n\n`template-frame-map.json` must include:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"outputSlides\": [\n    {\n      \"outputSlide\": 1,\n      \"sourceSlide\": 3,\n      \"narrativeRole\": \"opening thesis\",\n      \"reuseMode\": \"duplicate-slide\",\n      \"editTargets\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"omittedSourceSlides\": [\n    { \"sourceSlide\": 4, \"reason\": \"appendix pattern not needed\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nEvery output slide requires a `sourceSlide`. Source slides may be reused multiple\ntimes. If a source slide is omitted from the final narrative, record why in the\naudit or frame map.\n\n## Build The Starter Deck\n\nAfter `template-frame-map.json` is complete, run:\n\n```bash\nnode \"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/prepare_template_starter_deck.mjs\" \\\n  --workspace \"$TMP_DIR\" \\\n  --pptx \"<source.pptx>\" \\\n  --map \"$TMP_DIR/template-frame-map.json\" \\\n  --out \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter.pptx\" \\\n  --preview-dir \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-preview\" \\\n  --layout-dir \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-layout\" \\\n  --contact-sheet \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-contact-sheet.png\"\n```\n\nThe starter deck script calls `validate_template_plan.mjs` to validate\n`template-frame-map.json` before duplication. For template-following,\n`editTargets` must resolve to inherited source elements with `shapeId`,\n`shapeIds`, `sourceElementId`, or `sourceElementIds`.\n`action: \"add\"` is rejected by default because it usually creates new content\nover copied placeholders. It also never counts as clearing a placeholder:\nevery inherited structural placeholder, including empty PowerPoint placeholders\nwith OOXML `<p:ph>` metadata and no visible text in the rendered PNG, must be\nfilled through the inherited element or explicitly deleted. If the chosen source\nslide lacks usable inherited slots, remap to another source slide or report a\nblocker.\n\nUse the starter PPTX as the authoring base. Edit copied placeholders, textboxes,\ncharts, tables, and images by placeholder or resolved element IDs from\n`template-inspect.ndjson` whenever possible. Fill inherited component slots; do\nnot lay a parallel custom design over the copied template slide.\n\nFor mapped source slides, do not use `presentation.slides.add()` to build a new\nslide. Import the copied/starter PPTX with artifact-tool, edit the existing\nduplicated slides, and export through `PresentationFile.exportPptx`.\n\nIf a copied slide cannot be edited cleanly, choose a different source slide and\nrerun the starter deck script. If no source slide can support the requested\ncontent, report the blocker and the closest source slide options.\n\n## Edit Rules\n\n`editTargets: []` means preserve-only. Do not add narrative text, charts,\ntables, images, panels, callouts, or boxes to that slide unless the validated\nframe map explicitly allows a new primitive with a bounded zone and reason. New\nprimitives must not cover inherited template content.\n\nBrand, logo, bumper, divider, separator, chrome, section, and blank slides are\npreserve-only patterns unless the source slide contains inherited content slots\nthat are explicitly filled or deleted. Do not use the large empty brand side of\na bumper as a canvas just because it has visual whitespace.\n\n## Placeholder Clearing Rules\n\nDefault every inherited shape, image, table, chart, layout element, master\nelement, and text object to `keep`. Only rewrite, clear, or delete an inherited\nobject when it is explicitly classified as `rewrite`, `replace`, or `delete` in\nthe slide's `template-frame-map.json` `editTargets` or in a separate edit plan\nderived from `template-inspect.ndjson` / layout JSON.\n\nActual placeholders are structural PowerPoint placeholders, such as elements\nwith resolved placeholder metadata or OOXML `<p:ph>` entries, and source objects\nexplicitly marked for editing in the map/edit plan. Visible sample content such\nas `Name goes here`, `Title goes here`, `Slide Number`, `Date`, `Footer`, and\ntemplate instruction notes may be cleared only when that specific object is\nclassified as a placeholder or authoring note.\n\nEmpty structural placeholders still count. If a copied shape has placeholder\nmetadata but no text, classify it in the edit plan and handle it with\n`rewrite`, `rewrite-and-reposition`, `replace`, `delete`, or\n`fill-placeholder`. `keep` and `add` do not satisfy placeholder handling because\nPowerPoint can show default edit-mode prompts such as `Click to add title` even\nwhen slide PNG renders look clean.\n\nDo not clear text by broad text heuristics. Never run logic equivalent to\n`if (text.trim()) shape.text = \"\"`, and never blank every text-bearing shape on\na copied slide. OpenAI wordmarks, brand text, page chrome, footers, source rails,\nsection markers, and master/layout furniture may be editable text objects; keep\nthem unless the edit plan explicitly says to change or delete that exact object.\n\nFor data edits:\n\n- Compute first, design second.\n- Show formulas or calculation definitions in notes or appendix when useful.\n- Rank and conclude from the computed result, not visual intuition.\n- Insert the result into an inherited table/chart/metric frame whenever\n  practical.\n\nFor media edits:\n\n- Verify identity/source before using headshots or logos.\n- Never replace missing logos, app icons, mascots, or product UI with\n  hand-drawn lookalikes or pseudo-official marks.\n- Normalize crops, background treatment, and image size.\n- Do not damage existing slide alignment.\n\nFor new real-world subjects, products, screenshots, people, places, events, or\nevidence, resolve public or official raster assets and swap them into inherited\nframes. Do not generate fake screenshots, fake UI, fake logos, fake product\nimages, fake evidence, or generated approximations of real entities.\n\n## QA\n\n1. Export slide PNGs, layout JSON, PPTX, and the montage with artifact-tool. The\n   canonical presentation artifact export writes per-slide renders and a montage\n   by default. Set `montage: false` only if a montage is not useful for this\n   run.\n2. Review each slide against the mapped source pattern, `content.json` or the\n   content plan, and rendered output.\n3. Check:\n   - hierarchy and primary read,\n   - text clipping and overflow,\n   - font resolution,\n   - chart/table legibility,\n   - image crop and mask fidelity,\n   - source accuracy,\n   - spacing rhythm,\n   - slide-to-slide pacing,\n   - editable token and asset structure,\n   - no blanket text clearing or unplanned deletion of brand chrome, logos,\n     footers, page markers, master/layout furniture, or source rails,\n   - no unfilled inherited placeholders, including `sldNum`, `dt`, `ftr`, or\n     visible default prompt text such as `Slide Number`, `Date`, or `Footer`.\n     This placeholder check must inspect the final exported PPTX XML, not only\n     rendered PNGs. For every `ppt/slides/slide*.xml`, fail QA if any `<p:sp>`\n     containing `<p:ph>` has an empty or whitespace-only text body, unless that\n     exact placeholder is intentionally filled or deleted in the edit plan. Do\n     not pass this gate by overlaying new text boxes on top of empty\n     placeholders.\n   - required inherited logos and brand marks are visible in the final exported\n     PPTX render. If a required logo from the source template is missing after\n     import/export, place the authentic extracted source asset onto the slide\n     itself.\n4. Before delivery, run:\n\n```bash\nnode \"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/check_template_fidelity.mjs\" \\\n  --workspace \"$TMP_DIR\" \\\n  --starter-pptx \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter.pptx\" \\\n  --final-pptx \"$FINAL_PPTX\" \\\n  --map \"$TMP_DIR/template-frame-map.json\" \\\n  --starter-layout-dir \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-layout\" \\\n  --final-layout-dir \"$LAYOUT_DIR/final\" \\\n  --edit-dir \"$TMP_DIR\"\n```\n\nIf it fails, fix the map or edit inherited elements directly. Do not switch to\noverlays, visual rebuilds, Python PPTX mutation, or direct OOXML mutation.\n\n5. Run one slide-scoped QA review per slide for multi-slide decks, then\n   integrate the findings into a final polish pass.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2000},"formatted_output":"# Content Quality and Narrative Rules\n\nRead this file before planning or authoring a presentation.\n\n## Define the communication job\n\nBefore selecting slides, layouts, or visuals, identify:\n\n- The audience and what they already know.\n- The deck's purpose: inform, teach, persuade, facilitate, recommend, or support\n  a decision.\n- The response the audience should have by the end: understand, believe,\n  decide, discuss, or do something.\n- The central takeaway the audience should retain.\n- Every content requirement and source constraint in the user's request.\n\nExpress the communication job in one sentence:\n\n> By the end, this audience should understand, believe, decide, discuss, or do\n> **X** because **Y**.\n\nFor an exploratory, educational, or neutral deck, define the understanding or\nquestions the audience should leave with instead of forcing a recommendation.\n\n## Choose an appropriate narrative arc\n\nChoose a structure that fits the communication job. Examples include:\n\n- Context -> stakes -> evidence -> implications -> action.\n- Question -> analysis -> answer.\n- Problem -> causes -> options -> recommendation.\n- Current state -> change -> future state.\n- Chronological sequence or process.\n- Concept -> example -> application.\n- Claim -> supporting evidence -> consequence.\n\nAn agenda or collection of sections is not by itself a narrative. The sequence\nshould be cumulative: each section should create the need for the next one.\n\nDo not force an emotional or persuasive arc onto a technical or reference deck.\nTechnical decks can build through logic, causality, chronology, learning\nprogression, or decision flow.\n\n## Make every slide advance the story\n\n- Give each slide one narrative job and one primary claim.\n- Prefer takeaway-style titles that communicate the slide's point rather than\n  merely naming its topic.\n- Order slides so that each one answers a question raised by the prior slide or\n  introduces a question the next slide resolves.\n- Add an explicit transition, bridge, or section pivot when the logic would\n  otherwise jump.\n- Integrate every requested topic into the narrative instead of appending\n  isolated requirement-coverage slides.\n- Remove repeated beats, redundant summaries, and content that does not advance\n  the throughline.\n- When following an existing template, preserve its title and layout\n  conventions. Strengthen the narrative through sequencing and copy rather than\n  breaking the template.\n\n## Turn evidence into meaning\n\nDo not present facts, metrics, charts, examples, or visuals as an inventory.\nFor each important piece of evidence, make clear:\n\n1. What the audience is seeing.\n2. What it means.\n3. Why it matters to this audience.\n4. How it changes the conclusion, decision, discussion, or next step.\n\nCharts and visuals must reinforce the claim being made at that point in the\nstory. Do not use an image merely to decorate a slide or use a chart without\ninterpreting its significance.\n\nUse grounded examples, scenarios, case studies, or human impact when they\nmaterially improve understanding and are appropriate to the subject. Never\ninvent people, quotes, facts, data, or case-study outcomes to make the story\nmore engaging.\n\n## Open and close deliberately\n\nThe opening should orient the audience with the relevant context, purpose,\nstakes, question, or tension. It should make clear why the deck deserves\nattention.\n\nThe ending should resolve the opening and match the deck's purpose:\n\n- Decision decks end with a recommendation, decision, or explicit ask.\n- Action decks end with owners, next steps, or commitments.\n- Educational decks end with synthesis or application.\n- Exploratory and facilitation decks end with implications and productive\n  questions without prematurely forcing a conclusion.\n\nDo not end abruptly on a detail slide, technical artifact, unframed summary, or\ngeneric \"Thank you\" slide.\n\n## Write natural, audience-facing copy\n\nVisible slide copy must serve the intended audience. Do not expose the process\nused to create the deck, instructions to the model, private planning notes,\npresenter tips, talk tracks, or implementation details unless the user\nexplicitly asks for them in the deliverable.\n\nTreat a requested presentation duration as a planning constraint for scope,\nslide count, and pacing. Do not convert it into visible timestamps or time\nranges in slide titles. Include timings only when the user explicitly requests\na timed agenda, run of show, facilitator guide, or similar audience-facing\nschedule. When timings are useful only to the presenter, keep them in speaker\nnotes or separate facilitator material rather than visible slide titles.\n\nWhen the user says that a concept is irrelevant, out of scope, or should be\nremoved, remove it. Do not preserve the content in a slide that announces that\nthe concept is irrelevant, omitted, or outside the presentation. Include an\nexplicit scope boundary only when the audience needs it or the user requests\none.\n\nWrite titles and body copy in direct, specific, natural language:\n\n- Prefer concrete claims over vague slogans.\n- Avoid formulaic transformations such as \"From X to Y\" unless that contrast is\n  genuinely the clearest description of the change.\n- Do not use colons, semicolons, em dashes, or parallel constructions as a\n  repeated sentence template.\n- Avoid compressed fragments that resemble advertising taglines rather than\n  complete thoughts.\n- Replace jargon, abstract noun clusters, and inflated phrasing with concrete\n  nouns and active verbs.\n- Remove duplicated qualifiers and repeated concepts.\n- Vary sentence structure naturally instead of forcing every title or bullet\n  into the same rhetorical pattern.\n\nA title should communicate a point a human presenter would plausibly say aloud.\nIf it sounds like a prompt, production note, slogan generator, or mechanical\ntemplate, rewrite it.\n# Template-Following Mode\n\nUse when the user provides an existing PPTX, asks to follow a presentation, or\nattaches a PPTX that is clearly or implicitly a template.\n\nThis is the entire template-following mode. Do not run template codegen, do not\nbuild or consume a reusable template registry, and do not rebuild a fresh deck\nfrom palette, fonts, screenshots, or vibes. Use a source slide inventory only:\ninspect every source slide, choose source slides for the requested output,\nduplicate those source slides, and edit the copied elements in place.\n\nStore every template-following intermediate named in this reference under\n`$TMP_DIR`. Only `FINAL_PPTX` may be written outside `$TMP_DIR`.\n\n## Exact Clone/Edit Contract\n\n1. Copy/import the source PPTX.\n2. Inspect every source slide render and layout.\n3. Create `template-frame-map.json` mapping every output slide to a source\n   slide.\n4. Build `template-starter.pptx` by duplicating mapped source slides.\n5. Apply the requested edits while preserving the source deck's structure and\n   styling.\n6. Preserve the source template's typography exactly: keep original font\n   family, font size, weight, line spacing, paragraph spacing, text insets,\n   alignment, and vertical anchor for every edited text box/table cell unless\n   the user explicitly asks to restyle or resize. If new copy does not fit,\n   shorten the copy, choose a more suitable source layout, or split content\n   across another cloned slide; do not silently shrink text to make it fit.\n7. Audit inherited placeholders, including `sldNum`, `dt`, and `ftr`, and\n   either fill them intentionally or delete them. Treat visible default prompt\n   text such as `Slide Number`, `Date`, and `Footer` as an empty inherited\n   placeholder; never leave empty PowerPoint placeholders in the final deck,\n   even if PNG renders hide them.\n8. Export PNGs, layout JSON, and PPTX for QA.\n9. Deliver the edited copy and mention the sources cited or used.\n\nThe production load/export path is artifact-tool:\n\n```js\nconst presentation = await PresentationFile.importPptx(await FileBlob.load(sourcePptx));\nconst pptx = await PresentationFile.exportPptx(presentation);\nawait pptx.save(outputPptx);\n```\n\nUse artifact-tool and headless package tooling only. If artifact-tool cannot\ninspect, duplicate, render, or export the source deck, report the blocker. Do\nnot fall back to a theme-matched rebuild.\n\n## Source Slide Inventory\n\nBefore planning final copy, run:\n\n```bash\nnode \"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/inspect_template_deck.mjs\" \\\n  --workspace \"$TMP_DIR\" \\\n  --pptx \"<source.pptx>\"\n```\n\nReview all source slide PNGs, layout JSON files, `template-inspect.ndjson`,\nextracted media, font evidence, and `template-manifest.json`. Do not sample only\none or two representative slides.\n\nThen create under `$TMP_DIR`:\n\n- `template-audit.txt`: source structure, reusable slide types, inherited\n  placeholders, brand/assets, typography/spacing rules, and insertion contract.\n- `template-frame-map.json`: the full source slide inventory and the selected\n  source slide for every output slide.\n- `deviation-log.txt`: each intentional departure from a copied source slide,\n  with reason and affected slides.\n\n`template-frame-map.json` must include:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"outputSlides\": [\n    {\n      \"outputSlide\": 1,\n      \"sourceSlide\": 3,\n      \"narrativeRole\": \"opening thesis\",\n      \"reuseMode\": \"duplicate-slide\",\n      \"editTargets\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"omittedSourceSlides\": [\n    { \"sourceSlide\": 4, \"reason\": \"appendix pattern not needed\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nEvery output slide requires a `sourceSlide`. Source slides may be reused multiple\ntimes. If a source slide is omitted from the final narrative, record why in the\naudit or frame map.\n\n## Build The Starter Deck\n\nAfter `template-frame-map.json` is complete, run:\n\n```bash\nnode \"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/prepare_template_starter_deck.mjs\" \\\n  --workspace \"$TMP_DIR\" \\\n  --pptx \"<source.pptx>\" \\\n  --map \"$TMP_DIR/template-frame-map.json\" \\\n  --out \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter.pptx\" \\\n  --preview-dir \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-preview\" \\\n  --layout-dir \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-layout\" \\\n  --contact-sheet \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-contact-sheet.png\"\n```\n\nThe starter deck script calls `validate_template_plan.mjs` to validate\n`template-frame-map.json` before duplication. For template-following,\n`editTargets` must resolve to inherited source elements with `shapeId`,\n`shapeIds`, `sourceElementId`, or `sourceElementIds`.\n`action: \"add\"` is rejected by default because it usually creates new content\nover copied placeholders. It also never counts as clearing a placeholder:\nevery inherited structural placeholder, including empty PowerPoint placeholders\nwith OOXML `<p:ph>` metadata and no visible text in the rendered PNG, must be\nfilled through the inherited element or explicitly deleted. If the chosen source\nslide lacks usable inherited slots, remap to another source slide or report a\nblocker.\n\nUse the starter PPTX as the authoring base. Edit copied placeholders, textboxes,\ncharts, tables, and images by placeholder or resolved element IDs from\n`template-inspect.ndjson` whenever possible. Fill inherited component slots; do\nnot lay a parallel custom design over the copied template slide.\n\nFor mapped source slides, do not use `presentation.slides.add()` to build a new\nslide. Import the copied/starter PPTX with artifact-tool, edit the existing\nduplicated slides, and export through `PresentationFile.exportPptx`.\n\nIf a copied slide cannot be edited cleanly, choose a different source slide and\nrerun the starter deck script. If no source slide can support the requested\ncontent, report the blocker and the closest source slide options.\n\n## Edit Rules\n\n`editTargets: []` means preserve-only. Do not add narrative text, charts,\ntables, images, panels, callouts, or boxes to that slide unless the validated\nframe map explicitly allows a new primitive with a bounded zone and reason. New\nprimitives must not cover inherited template content.\n\nBrand, logo, bumper, divider, separator, chrome, section, and blank slides are\npreserve-only patterns unless the source slide contains inherited content slots\nthat are explicitly filled or deleted. Do not use the large empty brand side of\na bumper as a canvas just because it has visual whitespace.\n\n## Placeholder Clearing Rules\n\nDefault every inherited shape, image, table, chart, layout element, master\nelement, and text object to `keep`. Only rewrite, clear, or delete an inherited\nobject when it is explicitly classified as `rewrite`, `replace`, or `delete` in\nthe slide's `template-frame-map.json` `editTargets` or in a separate edit plan\nderived from `template-inspect.ndjson` / layout JSON.\n\nActual placeholders are structural PowerPoint placeholders, such as elements\nwith resolved placeholder metadata or OOXML `<p:ph>` entries, and source objects\nexplicitly marked for editing in the map/edit plan. Visible sample content such\nas `Name goes here`, `Title goes here`, `Slide Number`, `Date`, `Footer`, and\ntemplate instruction notes may be cleared only when that specific object is\nclassified as a placeholder or authoring note.\n\nEmpty structural placeholders still count. If a copied shape has placeholder\nmetadata but no text, classify it in the edit plan and handle it with\n`rewrite`, `rewrite-and-reposition`, `replace`, `delete`, or\n`fill-placeholder`. `keep` and `add` do not satisfy placeholder handling because\nPowerPoint can show default edit-mode prompts such as `Click to add title` even\nwhen slide PNG renders look clean.\n\nDo not clear text by broad text heuristics. Never run logic equivalent to\n`if (text.trim()) shape.text = \"\"`, and never blank every text-bearing shape on\na copied slide. OpenAI wordmarks, brand text, page chrome, footers, source rails,\nsection markers, and master/layout furniture may be editable text objects; keep\nthem unless the edit plan explicitly says to change or delete that exact object.\n\nFor data edits:\n\n- Compute first, design second.\n- Show formulas or calculation definitions in notes or appendix when useful.\n- Rank and conclude from the computed result, not visual intuition.\n- Insert the result into an inherited table/chart/metric frame whenever\n  practical.\n\nFor media edits:\n\n- Verify identity/source before using headshots or logos.\n- Never replace missing logos, app icons, mascots, or product UI with\n  hand-drawn lookalikes or pseudo-official marks.\n- Normalize crops, background treatment, and image size.\n- Do not damage existing slide alignment.\n\nFor new real-world subjects, products, screenshots, people, places, events, or\nevidence, resolve public or official raster assets and swap them into inherited\nframes. Do not generate fake screenshots, fake UI, fake logos, fake product\nimages, fake evidence, or generated approximations of real entities.\n\n## QA\n\n1. Export slide PNGs, layout JSON, PPTX, and the montage with artifact-tool. The\n   canonical presentation artifact export writes per-slide renders and a montage\n   by default. Set `montage: false` only if a montage is not useful for this\n   run.\n2. Review each slide against the mapped source pattern, `content.json` or the\n   content plan, and rendered output.\n3. Check:\n   - hierarchy and primary read,\n   - text clipping and overflow,\n   - font resolution,\n   - chart/table legibility,\n   - image crop and mask fidelity,\n   - source accuracy,\n   - spacing rhythm,\n   - slide-to-slide pacing,\n   - editable token and asset structure,\n   - no blanket text clearing or unplanned deletion of brand chrome, logos,\n     footers, page markers, master/layout furniture, or source rails,\n   - no unfilled inherited placeholders, including `sldNum`, `dt`, `ftr`, or\n     visible default prompt text such as `Slide Number`, `Date`, or `Footer`.\n     This placeholder check must inspect the final exported PPTX XML, not only\n     rendered PNGs. For every `ppt/slides/slide*.xml`, fail QA if any `<p:sp>`\n     containing `<p:ph>` has an empty or whitespace-only text body, unless that\n     exact placeholder is intentionally filled or deleted in the edit plan. Do\n     not pass this gate by overlaying new text boxes on top of empty\n     placeholders.\n   - required inherited logos and brand marks are visible in the final exported\n     PPTX render. If a required logo from the source template is missing after\n     import/export, place the authentic extracted source asset onto the slide\n     itself.\n4. Before delivery, run:\n\n```bash\nnode \"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/check_template_fidelity.mjs\" \\\n  --workspace \"$TMP_DIR\" \\\n  --starter-pptx \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter.pptx\" \\\n  --final-pptx \"$FINAL_PPTX\" \\\n  --map \"$TMP_DIR/template-frame-map.json\" \\\n  --starter-layout-dir \"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-layout\" \\\n  --final-layout-dir \"$LAYOUT_DIR/final\" \\\n  --edit-dir \"$TMP_DIR\"\n```\n\nIf it fails, fix the map or edit inherited elements directly. Do not switch to\noverlays, visual rebuilds, Python PPTX mutation, or direct OOXML mutation.\n\n5. Run one slide-scoped QA review per slide for multi-slide decks, then\n   integrate the findings into a final polish pass.\n"},"started_at_ms":1789292180891,"completed_at_ms":1789292180891}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T09:36:21.029Z","ordinal":4444,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a09554-4029-7852-babe-0cbdfd76c3e6","turn_id":"01a09a1f-5842-7af1-ac12-7cf624fbc8c9","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-0612cfa7-f543-4daa-ac87-16541ecda827","process_id":"88184","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat web/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/02-guides/testing/vitest.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/luisdehlwes/Developer/02_events/04_zurich/ehl-zurich-hackathon-submission","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat web/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/02-guides/testing/vitest.md","name":"vitest.md","path":"web/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/02-guides/testing/vitest.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\ntitle: How to set up Vitest with Next.js\nnav_title: Vitest\ndescription: Learn how to set up Vitest with Next.js for Unit Testing.\n---\n\nVitest and React Testing Library are frequently used together for **Unit Testing**. This guide will show you how to set up Vitest with Next.js and write your first tests.\n\n> **Good to know:** Since `async` Server Components are new to the React ecosystem, Vitest currently does not support them. While you can still run **unit tests** for synchronous Server and Client Components, we recommend using **E2E tests** for `async` components.\n\n## Quickstart\n\nYou can use `create-next-app` with the Next.js [with-vitest](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest) example to quickly get started:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\npnpm create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\nnpx create-next-app@latest --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\nyarn create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\nbun create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n## Manual Setup\n\nTo manually set up Vitest, install `vitest` and the following packages as dev dependencies:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\n# Using TypeScript\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\n# Using TypeScript\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\n# Using TypeScript\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\n# Using TypeScript\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\nCreate a `vitest.config.mts|js` file in the root of your project, and add the following options:\n\n```ts filename=\"vitest.config.mts\" switcher\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport tsconfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tsconfigPaths(), react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```js filename=\"vitest.config.js\" switcher\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor more information on configuring Vitest, please refer to the [Vitest Configuration](https://vitest.dev/config/#configuration) docs.\n\nThen, add a `test` script to your `package.json`:\n\n```json filename=\"package.json\"\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"next dev\",\n    \"build\": \"next build\",\n    \"start\": \"next start\",\n    \"test\": \"vitest\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen you run `npm run test`, Vitest will **watch** for changes in your project by default.\n\n## Creating your first Vitest Unit Test\n\nCheck that everything is working by creating a test to check if the `<Page />` component successfully renders a heading:\n\n<AppOnly>\n\n```tsx filename=\"app/page.tsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"app/page.jsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx filename=\"__tests__/page.test.tsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../app/page'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"__tests__/page.test.jsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../app/page'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n> **Good to know**: The example above uses the common `__tests__` convention, but test files can also be colocated inside the `app` router.\n\n</AppOnly>\n\n<PagesOnly>\n\n```tsx filename=\"pages/index.tsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"pages/index.jsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx filename=\"__tests__/index.test.tsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"__tests__/index.test.jsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n</PagesOnly>\n\n## Running your tests\n\nThen, run the following command to run your tests:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\npnpm test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\nnpm run test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\nyarn test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\nbun run test\n```\n\n## Additional Resources\n\nYou may find these resources helpful:\n\n- [Next.js with Vitest example](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest)\n- [Vitest Docs](https://vitest.dev/guide/)\n- [React Testing Library Docs](https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro/)\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\ntitle: How to set up Vitest with Next.js\nnav_title: Vitest\ndescription: Learn how to set up Vitest with Next.js for Unit Testing.\n---\n\nVitest and React Testing Library are frequently used together for **Unit Testing**. This guide will show you how to set up Vitest with Next.js and write your first tests.\n\n> **Good to know:** Since `async` Server Components are new to the React ecosystem, Vitest currently does not support them. While you can still run **unit tests** for synchronous Server and Client Components, we recommend using **E2E tests** for `async` components.\n\n## Quickstart\n\nYou can use `create-next-app` with the Next.js [with-vitest](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest) example to quickly get started:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\npnpm create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\nnpx create-next-app@latest --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\nyarn create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\nbun create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n## Manual Setup\n\nTo manually set up Vitest, install `vitest` and the following packages as dev dependencies:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\n# Using TypeScript\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\n# Using TypeScript\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\n# Using TypeScript\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\n# Using TypeScript\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\nCreate a `vitest.config.mts|js` file in the root of your project, and add the following options:\n\n```ts filename=\"vitest.config.mts\" switcher\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport tsconfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tsconfigPaths(), react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```js filename=\"vitest.config.js\" switcher\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor more information on configuring Vitest, please refer to the [Vitest Configuration](https://vitest.dev/config/#configuration) docs.\n\nThen, add a `test` script to your `package.json`:\n\n```json filename=\"package.json\"\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"next dev\",\n    \"build\": \"next build\",\n    \"start\": \"next start\",\n    \"test\": \"vitest\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen you run `npm run test`, Vitest will **watch** for changes in your project by default.\n\n## Creating your first Vitest Unit Test\n\nCheck that everything is working by creating a test to check if the `<Page />` component successfully renders a heading:\n\n<AppOnly>\n\n```tsx filename=\"app/page.tsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"app/page.jsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx filename=\"__tests__/page.test.tsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../app/page'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"__tests__/page.test.jsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../app/page'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n> **Good to know**: The example above uses the common `__tests__` convention, but test files can also be colocated inside the `app` router.\n\n</AppOnly>\n\n<PagesOnly>\n\n```tsx filename=\"pages/index.tsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"pages/index.jsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx filename=\"__tests__/index.test.tsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"__tests__/index.test.jsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n</PagesOnly>\n\n## Running your tests\n\nThen, run the following command to run your tests:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\npnpm test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\nnpm run test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\nyarn test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\nbun run test\n```\n\n## Additional Resources\n\nYou may find these resources helpful:\n\n- [Next.js with Vitest example](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest)\n- [Vitest Docs](https://vitest.dev/guide/)\n- [React Testing Library Docs](https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro/)\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1458},"formatted_output":"---\ntitle: How to set up Vitest with Next.js\nnav_title: Vitest\ndescription: Learn how to set up Vitest with Next.js for Unit Testing.\n---\n\nVitest and React Testing Library are frequently used together for **Unit Testing**. This guide will show you how to set up Vitest with Next.js and write your first tests.\n\n> **Good to know:** Since `async` Server Components are new to the React ecosystem, Vitest currently does not support them. While you can still run **unit tests** for synchronous Server and Client Components, we recommend using **E2E tests** for `async` components.\n\n## Quickstart\n\nYou can use `create-next-app` with the Next.js [with-vitest](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest) example to quickly get started:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\npnpm create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\nnpx create-next-app@latest --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\nyarn create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\nbun create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\n```\n\n## Manual Setup\n\nTo manually set up Vitest, install `vitest` and the following packages as dev dependencies:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\n# Using TypeScript\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\n# Using TypeScript\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\n# Using TypeScript\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\n# Using TypeScript\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n# Using JavaScript\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\n```\n\nCreate a `vitest.config.mts|js` file in the root of your project, and add the following options:\n\n```ts filename=\"vitest.config.mts\" switcher\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\nimport tsconfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tsconfigPaths(), react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```js filename=\"vitest.config.js\" switcher\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor more information on configuring Vitest, please refer to the [Vitest Configuration](https://vitest.dev/config/#configuration) docs.\n\nThen, add a `test` script to your `package.json`:\n\n```json filename=\"package.json\"\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"next dev\",\n    \"build\": \"next build\",\n    \"start\": \"next start\",\n    \"test\": \"vitest\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen you run `npm run test`, Vitest will **watch** for changes in your project by default.\n\n## Creating your first Vitest Unit Test\n\nCheck that everything is working by creating a test to check if the `<Page />` component successfully renders a heading:\n\n<AppOnly>\n\n```tsx filename=\"app/page.tsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"app/page.jsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx filename=\"__tests__/page.test.tsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../app/page'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"__tests__/page.test.jsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../app/page'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n> **Good to know**: The example above uses the common `__tests__` convention, but test files can also be colocated inside the `app` router.\n\n</AppOnly>\n\n<PagesOnly>\n\n```tsx filename=\"pages/index.tsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"pages/index.jsx\" switcher\nimport Link from 'next/link'\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Home</h1>\n      <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx filename=\"__tests__/index.test.tsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n```jsx filename=\"__tests__/index.test.jsx\" switcher\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\n\ntest('Page', () => {\n  render(<Page />)\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\n})\n```\n\n</PagesOnly>\n\n## Running your tests\n\nThen, run the following command to run your tests:\n\n```bash package=\"pnpm\"\npnpm test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"npm\"\nnpm run test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"yarn\"\nyarn test\n```\n\n```bash package=\"bun\"\nbun run test\n```\n\n## Additional Resources\n\nYou may find these resources helpful:\n\n- [Next.js with Vitest example](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest)\n- [Vitest Docs](https://vitest.dev/guide/)\n- [React Testing Library Docs](https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro/)\n"},"started_at_ms":1789292181028,"completed_at_ms":1789292181029}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T09:36:22.253Z","ordinal":4445,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a09a1f-faed-77c3-ab1e-faef9f980dac","call_id":"call_nNmpnNEvC1jNH0mRRZswidyS","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"6d0acf\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":4239,\"output\":\"# Content Quality and Narrative Rules\\n\\nRead this file before planning or authoring a presentation.\\n\\n## Define the communication job\\n\\nBefore selecting slides, layouts, or visuals, identify:\\n\\n- The audience and what they already know.\\n- The deck's purpose: inform, teach, persuade, facilitate, recommend, or support\\n  a decision.\\n- The response the audience should have by the end: understand, believe,\\n  decide, discuss, or do something.\\n- The central takeaway the audience should retain.\\n- Every content requirement and source constraint in the user's request.\\n\\nExpress the communication job in one sentence:\\n\\n> By the end, this audience should understand, believe, decide, discuss, or do\\n> **X** because **Y**.\\n\\nFor an exploratory, educational, or neutral deck, define the understanding or\\nquestions the audience should leave with instead of forcing a recommendation.\\n\\n## Choose an appropriate narrative arc\\n\\nChoose a structure that fits the communication job. Examples include:\\n\\n- Context -> stakes -> evidence -> implications -> action.\\n- Question -> analysis -> answer.\\n- Problem -> causes -> options -> recommendation.\\n- Current state -> change -> future state.\\n- Chronological sequence or process.\\n- Concept -> example -> application.\\n- Claim -> supporting evidence -> consequence.\\n\\nAn agenda or collection of sections is not by itself a narrative. The sequence\\nshould be cumulative: each section should create the need for the next one.\\n\\nDo not force an emotional or persuasive arc onto a technical or reference deck.\\nTechnical decks can build through logic, causality, chronology, learning\\nprogression, or decision flow.\\n\\n## Make every slide advance the story\\n\\n- Give each slide one narrative job and one primary claim.\\n- Prefer takeaway-style titles that communicate the slide's point rather than\\n  merely naming its topic.\\n- Order slides so that each one answers a question raised by the prior slide or\\n  introduces a question the next slide resolves.\\n- Add an explicit transition, bridge, or section pivot when the logic would\\n  otherwise jump.\\n- Integrate every requested topic into the narrative instead of appending\\n  isolated requirement-coverage slides.\\n- Remove repeated beats, redundant summaries, and content that does not advance\\n  the throughline.\\n- When following an existing template, preserve its title and layout\\n  conventions. Strengthen the narrative through sequencing and copy rather than\\n  breaking the template.\\n\\n## Turn evidence into meaning\\n\\nDo not present facts, metrics, charts, examples, or visuals as an inventory.\\nFor each important piece of evidence, make clear:\\n\\n1. What the audience is seeing.\\n2. What it means.\\n3. Why it matters to this audience.\\n4. How it changes the conclusion, decision, discussion, or next step.\\n\\nCharts and visuals must reinforce the claim being made at that point in the\\nstory. Do not use an image merely to decorate a slide or use a chart without\\ninterpreting its significance.\\n\\nUse grounded examples, scenarios, case studies, or human impact when they\\nmaterially improve understanding and are appropriate to the subject. Never\\ninvent people, quotes, facts, data, or case-study outcomes to make the story\\nmore engaging.\\n\\n## Open and close deliberately\\n\\nThe opening should orient the audience with the relevant context, purpose,\\nstakes, question, or tension. It should make clear why the deck deserves\\nattention.\\n\\nThe ending should resolve the opening and match the deck's purpose:\\n\\n- Decision decks end with a recommendation, decision, or explicit ask.\\n- Action decks end with owners, next steps, or commitments.\\n- Educational decks end with synthesis or application.\\n- Exploratory and facilitation decks end with implications and productive\\n  questions without prematurely forcing a conclusion.\\n\\nDo not end abruptly on a detail slide, technical artifact, unframed summary, or\\ngeneric \\\"Thank you\\\" slide.\\n\\n## Write natural, audience-facing copy\\n\\nVisible slide copy must serve the intended audience. Do not expose the process\\nused to create the deck, instructions to the model, private planning notes,\\npresenter tips, talk tracks, or implementation details unless the user\\nexplicitly asks for them in the deliverable.\\n\\nTreat a requested presentation duration as a planning constraint for scope,\\nslide count, and pacing. Do not convert it into visible timestamps or time\\nranges in slide titles. Include timings only when the user explicitly requests\\na timed agenda, run of show, facilitator guide, or similar audience-facing\\nschedule. When timings are useful only to the presenter, keep them in speaker\\nnotes or separate facilitator material rather than visible slide titles.\\n\\nWhen the user says that a concept is irrelevant, out of scope, or should be\\nremoved, remove it. Do not preserve the content in a slide that announces that\\nthe concept is irrelevant, omitted, or outside the presentation. Include an\\nexplicit scope boundary only when the audience needs it or the user requests\\none.\\n\\nWrite titles and body copy in direct, specific, natural language:\\n\\n- Prefer concrete claims over vague slogans.\\n- Avoid formulaic transformations such as \\\"From X to Y\\\" unless that contrast is\\n  genuinely the clearest description of the change.\\n- Do not use colons, semicolons, em dashes, or parallel constructions as a\\n  repeated sentence template.\\n- Avoid compressed fragments that resemble advertising taglines rather than\\n  complete thoughts.\\n- Replace jargon, abstract noun clusters, and inflated phrasing with concrete\\n  nouns and active verbs.\\n- Remove duplicated qualifiers and repeated concepts.\\n- Vary sentence structure naturally instead of forcing every title or bullet\\n  into the same rhetorical pattern.\\n\\nA title should communicate a point a human presenter would plausibly say aloud.\\nIf it sounds like a prompt, production note, slogan generator, or mechanical\\ntemplate, rewrite it.\\n# Template-Following Mode\\n\\nUse when the user provides an existing PPTX, asks to follow a presentation, or\\nattaches a PPTX that is clearly or implicitly a template.\\n\\nThis is the entire template-following mode. Do not run template codegen, do not\\nbuild or consume a reusable template registry, and do not rebuild a fresh deck\\nfrom palette, fonts, screenshots, or vibes. Use a source slide inventory only:\\ninspect every source slide, choose source slides for the requested output,\\nduplicate those source slides, and edit the copied elements in place.\\n\\nStore every template-following intermediate named in this reference under\\n`$TMP_DIR`. Only `FINAL_PPTX` may be written outside `$TMP_DIR`.\\n\\n## Exact Clone/Edit Contract\\n\\n1. Copy/import the source PPTX.\\n2. Inspect every source slide render and layout.\\n3. Create `template-frame-map.json` mapping every output slide to a source\\n   slide.\\n4. Build `template-starter.pptx` by duplicating mapped source slides.\\n5. Apply the requested edits while preserving the source deck's structure and\\n   styling.\\n6. Preserve the source template's typography exactly: keep original font\\n   family, font size, weight, line spacing, paragraph spacing, text insets,\\n   alignment, and vertical anchor for every edited text box/table cell unless\\n   the user explicitly asks to restyle or resize. If new copy does not fit,\\n   shorten the copy, choose a more suitable source layout, or split content\\n   across another cloned slide; do not silently shrink text to make it fit.\\n7. Audit inherited placeholders, including `sldNum`, `dt`, and `ftr`, and\\n   either fill them intentionally or delete them. Treat visible default prompt\\n   text such as `Slide Number`, `Date`, and `Footer` as an empty inherited\\n   placeholder; never leave empty PowerPoint placeholders in the final deck,\\n   even if PNG renders hide them.\\n8. Export PNGs, layout JSON, and PPTX for QA.\\n9. Deliver the edited copy and mention the sources cited or used.\\n\\nThe production load/export path is artifact-tool:\\n\\n```js\\nconst presentation = await PresentationFile.importPptx(await FileBlob.load(sourcePptx));\\nconst pptx = await PresentationFile.exportPptx(presentation);\\nawait pptx.save(outputPptx);\\n```\\n\\nUse artifact-tool and headless package tooling only. If artifact-tool cannot\\ninspect, duplicate, render, or export the source deck, report the blocker. Do\\nnot fall back to a theme-matched rebuild.\\n\\n## Source Slide Inventory\\n\\nBefore planning final copy, run:\\n\\n```bash\\nnode \\\"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/inspect_template_deck.mjs\\\" \\\\\\n  --workspace \\\"$TMP_DIR\\\" \\\\\\n  --pptx \\\"<source.pptx>\\\"\\n```\\n\\nReview all source slide PNGs, layout JSON files, `template-inspect.ndjson`,\\nextracted media, font evidence, and `template-manifest.json`. Do not sample only\\none or two representative slides.\\n\\nThen create under `$TMP_DIR`:\\n\\n- `template-audit.txt`: source structure, reusable slide types, inherited\\n  placeholders, brand/assets, typography/spacing rules, and insertion contract.\\n- `template-frame-map.json`: the full source slide inventory and the selected\\n  source slide for every output slide.\\n- `deviation-log.txt`: each intentional departure from a copied source slide,\\n  with reason and affected slides.\\n\\n`template-frame-map.json` must include:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"outputSlides\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"outputSlide\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"sourceSlide\\\": 3,\\n      \\\"narrativeRole\\\": \\\"opening thesis\\\",\\n      \\\"reuseMode\\\": \\\"duplicate-slide\\\",\\n      \\\"editTargets\\\": []\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"omittedSourceSlides\\\": [\\n    { \\\"sourceSlide\\\": 4, \\\"reason\\\": \\\"appendix pattern not needed\\\" }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nEvery output slide requires a `sourceSlide`. Source slides may be reused multiple\\ntimes. If a source slide is omitted from the final narrative, record why in the\\naudit or frame map.\\n\\n## Build The Starter Deck\\n\\nAfter `template-frame-map.json` is complete, run:\\n\\n```bash\\nnode \\\"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/prepare_template_starter_deck.mjs\\\" \\\\\\n  --workspace \\\"$TMP_DIR\\\" \\\\\\n  --pptx \\\"<source.pptx>\\\" \\\\\\n  --map \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-frame-map.json\\\" \\\\\\n  --out \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-starter.pptx\\\" \\\\\\n  --preview-dir \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-preview\\\" \\\\\\n  --layout-dir \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-layout\\\" \\\\\\n  --contact-sheet \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-contact-sheet.png\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe starter deck script calls `validate_template_plan.mjs` to validate\\n`template-frame-map.json` before duplication. For template-following,\\n`editTargets` must resolve to inherited source elements with `shapeId`,\\n`shapeIds`, `sourceElementId`, or `sourceElementIds`.\\n`action: \\\"add\\\"` is rejected by default because it usually creates new content\\nover copied placeholders. It also never counts as clearing a placeholder:\\nevery inherited structural placeholder, including empty PowerPoint placeholders\\nwith OOXML `<p:ph>` metadata and no visible text in the rendered PNG, must be\\nfilled through the inherited element or explicitly deleted. If the chosen source\\nslide lacks usable inherited slots, remap to another source slide or report a\\nblocker.\\n\\nUse the starter PPTX as the authoring base. Edit copied placeholders, textboxes,\\ncharts, tables, and images by placeholder or resolved element IDs from\\n`template-inspect.ndjson` whenever possible. Fill inherited component slots; do\\nnot lay a parallel custom design over the copied template slide.\\n\\nFor mapped source slides, do not use `presentation.slides.add()` to build a new\\nslide. Import the copied/starter PPTX with artifact-tool, edit the existing\\nduplicated slides, and export through `PresentationFile.exportPptx`.\\n\\nIf a copied slide cannot be edited cleanly, choose a different source slide and\\nrerun the starter deck script. If no source slide can support the requested\\ncontent, report the blocker and the closest source slide options.\\n\\n## Edit Rules\\n\\n`editTargets: []` means preserve-only. Do not add narrative text, charts,\\ntables, images, panels, callouts, or boxes to that slide unless the validated\\nframe map explicitly allows a new primitive with a bounded zone and reason. New\\nprimitives must not cover inherited template content.\\n\\nBrand, logo, bumper, divider, separator, chrome, section, and blank slides are\\npreserve-only patterns unless the source slide contains inherited content slots\\nthat are explicitly filled or deleted. Do not use the large empty brand side of\\na bumper as a canvas just because it has visual whitespace.\\n\\n## Placeholder Clearing Rules\\n\\nDefault every inherited shape, image, table, chart, layout element, master\\nelement, and text object to `keep`. Only rewrite, clear, or delete an inherited\\nobject when it is explicitly classified as `rewrite`, `replace`, or `delete` in\\nthe slide's `template-frame-map.json` `editTargets` or in a separate edit plan\\nderived from `template-inspect.ndjson` / layout JSON.\\n\\nActual placeholders are structural PowerPoint placeholders, such as elements\\nwith resolved placeholder metadata or OOXML `<p:ph>` entries, and source objects\\nexplicitly marked for editing in the map/edit plan. Visible sample content such\\nas `Name goes here`, `Title goes here`, `Slide Number`, `Date`, `Footer`, and\\ntemplate instruction notes may be cleared only when that specific object is\\nclassified as a placeholder or authoring note.\\n\\nEmpty structural placeholders still count. If a copied shape has placeholder\\nmetadata but no text, classify it in the edit plan and handle it with\\n`rewrite`, `rewrite-and-reposition`, `replace`, `delete`, or\\n`fill-placeholder`. `keep` and `add` do not satisfy placeholder handling because\\nPowerPoint can show default edit-mode prompts such as `Click to add title` even\\nwhen slide PNG renders look clean.\\n\\nDo not clear text by broad text heuristics. Never run logic equivalent to\\n`if (text.trim()) shape.text = \\\"\\\"`, and never blank every text-bearing shape on\\na copied slide. OpenAI wordmarks, brand text, page chrome, footers, source rails,\\nsection markers, and master/layout furniture may be editable text objects; keep\\nthem unless the edit plan explicitly says to change or delete that exact object.\\n\\nFor data edits:\\n\\n- Compute first, design second.\\n- Show formulas or calculation definitions in notes or appendix when useful.\\n- Rank and conclude from the computed result, not visual intuition.\\n- Insert the result into an inherited table/chart/metric frame whenever\\n  practical.\\n\\nFor media edits:\\n\\n- Verify identity/source before using headshots or logos.\\n- Never replace missing logos, app icons, mascots, or product UI with\\n  hand-drawn lookalikes or pseudo-official marks.\\n- Normalize crops, background treatment, and image size.\\n- Do not damage existing slide alignment.\\n\\nFor new real-world subjects, products, screenshots, people, places, events, or\\nevidence, resolve public or official raster assets and swap them into inherited\\nframes. Do not generate fake screenshots, fake UI, fake logos, fake product\\nimages, fake evidence, or generated approximations of real entities.\\n\\n## QA\\n\\n1. Export slide PNGs, layout JSON, PPTX, and the montage with artifact-tool. The\\n   canonical presentation artifact export writes per-slide renders and a montage\\n   by default. Set `montage: false` only if a montage is not useful for this\\n   run.\\n2. Review each slide against the mapped source pattern, `content.json` or the\\n   content plan, and rendered output.\\n3. Check:\\n   - hierarchy and primary read,\\n   - text clipping and overflow,\\n   - font resolution,\\n   - chart/table legibility,\\n   - image crop and mask fidelity,\\n   - source accuracy,\\n   - spacing rhythm,\\n   - slide-to-slide pacing,\\n   - editable token and asset structure,\\n   - no blanket text clearing or unplanned deletion of brand chrome, logos,\\n     footers, page markers, master/layout furniture, or source rails,\\n   - no unfilled inherited placeholders, including `sldNum`, `dt`, `ftr`, or\\n     visible default prompt text such as `Slide Number`, `Date`, or `Footer`.\\n     This placeholder check must inspect the final exported PPTX XML, not only\\n     rendered PNGs. For every `ppt/slides/slide*.xml`, fail QA if any `<p:sp>`\\n     containing `<p:ph>` has an empty or whitespace-only text body, unless that\\n     exact placeholder is intentionally filled or deleted in the edit plan. Do\\n     not pass this gate by overlaying new text boxes on top of empty\\n     placeholders.\\n   - required inherited logos and brand marks are visible in the final exported\\n     PPTX render. If a required logo from the source template is missing after\\n     import/export, place the authentic extracted source asset onto the slide\\n     itself.\\n4. Before delivery, run:\\n\\n```bash\\nnode \\\"$SKILL_DIR/template_following_scripts/check_template_fidelity.mjs\\\" \\\\\\n  --workspace \\\"$TMP_DIR\\\" \\\\\\n  --starter-pptx \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-starter.pptx\\\" \\\\\\n  --final-pptx \\\"$FINAL_PPTX\\\" \\\\\\n  --map \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-frame-map.json\\\" \\\\\\n  --starter-layout-dir \\\"$TMP_DIR/template-starter-layout\\\" \\\\\\n  --final-layout-dir \\\"$LAYOUT_DIR/final\\\" \\\\\\n  --edit-dir \\\"$TMP_DIR\\\"\\n```\\n\\nIf it fails, fix the map or edit inherited elements directly. Do not switch to\\noverlays, visual rebuilds, Python PPTX mutation, or direct OOXML mutation.\\n\\n5. Run one slide-scoped QA review per slide for multi-slide decks, then\\n   integrate the findings into a final polish pass.\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"1eb7ab\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001458,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1572,\"output\":\"---\\ntitle: How to set up Vitest with Next.js\\nnav_title: Vitest\\ndescription: Learn how to set up Vitest with Next.js for Unit Testing.\\n---\\n\\nVitest and React Testing Library are frequently used together for **Unit Testing**. This guide will show you how to set up Vitest with Next.js and write your first tests.\\n\\n> **Good to know:** Since `async` Server Components are new to the React ecosystem, Vitest currently does not support them. While you can still run **unit tests** for synchronous Server and Client Components, we recommend using **E2E tests** for `async` components.\\n\\n## Quickstart\\n\\nYou can use `create-next-app` with the Next.js [with-vitest](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest) example to quickly get started:\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"pnpm\\\"\\npnpm create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"npm\\\"\\nnpx create-next-app@latest --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"yarn\\\"\\nyarn create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"bun\\\"\\nbun create next-app --example with-vitest with-vitest-app\\n```\\n\\n## Manual Setup\\n\\nTo manually set up Vitest, install `vitest` and the following packages as dev dependencies:\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"pnpm\\\"\\n# Using TypeScript\\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\\n# Using JavaScript\\npnpm add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"npm\\\"\\n# Using TypeScript\\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\\n# Using JavaScript\\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"yarn\\\"\\n# Using TypeScript\\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\\n# Using JavaScript\\nyarn add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"bun\\\"\\n# Using TypeScript\\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\\n# Using JavaScript\\nbun add -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom\\n```\\n\\nCreate a `vitest.config.mts|js` file in the root of your project, and add the following options:\\n\\n```ts filename=\\\"vitest.config.mts\\\" switcher\\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\\nimport tsconfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [tsconfigPaths(), react()],\\n  test: {\\n    environment: 'jsdom',\\n  },\\n})\\n```\\n\\n```js filename=\\\"vitest.config.js\\\" switcher\\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [react()],\\n  test: {\\n    environment: 'jsdom',\\n  },\\n})\\n```\\n\\nFor more information on configuring Vitest, please refer to the [Vitest Configuration](https://vitest.dev/config/#configuration) docs.\\n\\nThen, add a `test` script to your `package.json`:\\n\\n```json filename=\\\"package.json\\\"\\n{\\n  \\\"scripts\\\": {\\n    \\\"dev\\\": \\\"next dev\\\",\\n    \\\"build\\\": \\\"next build\\\",\\n    \\\"start\\\": \\\"next start\\\",\\n    \\\"test\\\": \\\"vitest\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWhen you run `npm run test`, Vitest will **watch** for changes in your project by default.\\n\\n## Creating your first Vitest Unit Test\\n\\nCheck that everything is working by creating a test to check if the `<Page />` component successfully renders a heading:\\n\\n<AppOnly>\\n\\n```tsx filename=\\\"app/page.tsx\\\" switcher\\nimport Link from 'next/link'\\n\\nexport default function Page() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Home</h1>\\n      <Link href=\\\"/about\\\">About</Link>\\n    </div>\\n  )\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```jsx filename=\\\"app/page.jsx\\\" switcher\\nimport Link from 'next/link'\\n\\nexport default function Page() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Home</h1>\\n      <Link href=\\\"/about\\\">About</Link>\\n    </div>\\n  )\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```tsx filename=\\\"__tests__/page.test.tsx\\\" switcher\\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\\nimport Page from '../app/page'\\n\\ntest('Page', () => {\\n  render(<Page />)\\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\\n})\\n```\\n\\n```jsx filename=\\\"__tests__/page.test.jsx\\\" switcher\\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\\nimport Page from '../app/page'\\n\\ntest('Page', () => {\\n  render(<Page />)\\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\\n})\\n```\\n\\n> **Good to know**: The example above uses the common `__tests__` convention, but test files can also be colocated inside the `app` router.\\n\\n</AppOnly>\\n\\n<PagesOnly>\\n\\n```tsx filename=\\\"pages/index.tsx\\\" switcher\\nimport Link from 'next/link'\\n\\nexport default function Page() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Home</h1>\\n      <Link href=\\\"/about\\\">About</Link>\\n    </div>\\n  )\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```jsx filename=\\\"pages/index.jsx\\\" switcher\\nimport Link from 'next/link'\\n\\nexport default function Page() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Home</h1>\\n      <Link href=\\\"/about\\\">About</Link>\\n    </div>\\n  )\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```tsx filename=\\\"__tests__/index.test.tsx\\\" switcher\\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\\n\\ntest('Page', () => {\\n  render(<Page />)\\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\\n})\\n```\\n\\n```jsx filename=\\\"__tests__/index.test.jsx\\\" switcher\\nimport { expect, test } from 'vitest'\\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'\\nimport Page from '../pages/index'\\n\\ntest('Page', () => {\\n  render(<Page />)\\n  expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1, name: 'Home' })).toBeDefined()\\n})\\n```\\n\\n</PagesOnly>\\n\\n## Running your tests\\n\\nThen, run the following command to run your tests:\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"pnpm\\\"\\npnpm test\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"npm\\\"\\nnpm run test\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"yarn\\\"\\nyarn test\\n```\\n\\n```bash package=\\\"bun\\\"\\nbun run test\\n```\\n\\n## Additional Resources\\n\\nYou may find these resources helpful:\\n\\n- [Next.js with Vitest example](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-vitest)\\n- [Vitest Docs](https://vitest.dev/guide/)\\n- [React Testing Library Docs](https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro/)\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"646e06\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.003601709,\"session_id\":41524,\"original_token_count\":66,\"output\":\"/var/folders/j4/4j7qn2vd4zd6ykklypxn1d540000gn/T/perigee-results.9Xj5K0Y1zD/tmp\\n(node:83740) ExperimentalWarning: localStorage is not available because --localstorage-file was not provided.\\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a09a1f-5842-7af1-ac12-7cf624fbc8c9","create_time":1789292182.253227}}}
